zendiloos (0.5.0~rc.1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Test release of 0.5.0. Not yet run on real NAS hardware for a while:
    for trying out, with data that has a backup elsewhere.
  * Virtual machines (preview, x86-64): an App Center plugin with libvirt
    and QEMU from Debian. VMs with several disks and network cards, an
    image library, a console in the browser and their load live, changed
    after they are made; storage locations on several pools; networks
    through a bridge, directly on a card or internal to the NAS; a log.
    Snapshots of all of a VM's disks at once, by hand and on schedules,
    rolled back while it is off; backups into a share or onto a USB drive,
    restored as a new VM or over the old one. A TPM and Secure Boot with
    Microsoft's keys for Windows 11; USB devices of the host handed
    through, PCI devices with their whole IOMMU group where it is on.
  * Backup plugin: rsync and rclone from Debian. Tasks from a share to
    another pool, a USB drive (also when it is plugged in), an rsync
    server through its daemon or over SSH with the host key pinned,
    Google Drive or OneDrive, and from a server into the NAS; mirrors and
    dated versions with retention; names and contents encrypted if
    wanted; restores of all or some of a version. Each run in a sandboxed
    unit, with its log and transfer list.
  * Network: zendiloOS manages the network with systemd-networkd also on
    installations from the ISO, and takes it over from ifupdown. Bridges,
    bonds and VLANs; every change stays only once confirmed and goes back
    by itself otherwise; a card is switched off only after a question.
    Changes made to the files outside zendiloOS are told, and restored or
    kept.
  * Configuration backup: zendiloOS's database, accounts, certificates and
    settings in one file encrypted with a passphrase, downloaded or on a
    schedule into a share or onto a USB drive, restored on a fresh
    installation instead of the first setup. The old installation's pools
    are offered for import, their shares mounted again; the network's and
    the firewall's settings come into force only once confirmed. The ISO's
    boot menu has a rescue entry.
  * The machine's name can be changed on the Info page; SMB, mDNS and
    WS-Discovery follow.
  * Certificates from Let's Encrypt through 81 DNS providers, each checked
    against Debian's acme.sh, IPv64 among them.
  * Reliability: notifications are delivered again when the network was
    not up yet, and update downloads tried again; a kernel without its
    ZFS module is never started; the wear of an eMMC system disk is
    watched; fewer writes to the system disk; systemd is reloaded only
    when something it reads changed. An update is no longer stopped by a
    removed kernel's modules, and a short check is no longer told as cut
    off.
  * arm64: packages and an installation ISO (UEFI), built and tested in
    CI; not yet tried on real arm64 hardware. Virtual machines are offered
    on x86-64 only. Each release carries a software bill of materials
    (CycloneDX) per architecture and the licences.
  * Storage: the new pool dialog is a wizard with estimates. Files: the
    explorer has an action toolbar, a grid view and sections.
  * Fixes and hardening throughout.

 -- zendiloOS <github.com@stuch.me>  Thu, 12 Nov 2026 14:54:46 +0000

zendiloos (0.4.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Firewall changes wait for confirmation on forwarded connections too,
    and the drop for forwarding from outside covers every Docker bridge,
    whatever its name and however new.
  * Recovery codes stay usable when someone with only the password used up
    the account's code limit; administrators can clear an account's failed
    sign-ins.
  * Certificates: every certificate of a new chain is checked, nginx tests
    it before it stays, and a renewal nginx did not take is shown and
    retried. The web server's saved settings are put in force again after
    a restart.
  * Apps and projects: the confirmation names host paths reached through
    named volumes, devices, zendiloOS's own folders and the host network;
    the catalog's policy applies again; accepting risks is bound to what
    was checked. Projects made elsewhere that bind into shares are not
    redeployed or updated through zendiloOS.
  * Moves off FAT and exFAT cards are reliable, and a move goes on into a
    folder of the same name at the target without replacing anything. A
    folder download whose files change while it is packed ends visibly
    short.
  * Ejected drives stay ejected across restarts until they are pulled out;
    disks in USB docks keep one inventory record.
  * Volume backups stay within bounded memory on very large volumes and
    leave no files behind when they fail late.
  * Updates: a release is installed only if its signature names its
    version and the NAS's channel takes it; an update cut off while dpkg
    configured can be finished from the Updates page; no jobs start while
    an update installs. Deploys share one time budget and keep their pins
    consistent; the image update schedule no longer skips a run.
  * Info and Notifications use the space of the window; the enclosure has
    a heading of its own; errors name disks as the pages do;
    administrators see every account's personal folder in the explorer.
  * Fixes and hardening throughout.

 -- zendiloOS <github.com@stuch.me>  Tue, 10 Nov 2026 12:43:05 +0000

zendiloos (0.3.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Access to a share is the share's as a whole: Samba, the file manager
    and NFS enforce its rules, and every share's files carry one ACL, for
    the group zendiloos-files. Changes of rules, groups and accounts take
    effect at once, without going through the files. Shares made with
    0.2.0 are converted once, in the background, one at a time.
  * NFS clients are named per host or network, read only or read and
    write, and every request is squashed to zendiloos-files.
  * Deleting over SMB or NFS in a share is final; the snapshots keep
    earlier states, and the file manager keeps its recycle bin.
  * Docker volumes are listed with their containers, backed up on a
    schedule into a shared folder and restored, under their own name or
    into another volume. Backups of deleted volumes stay restorable from
    a Backups page, and backups can be imported from a folder.
  * Disks show where they sit: an enclosure schematic, known NAS models,
    Locate and an inventory.
  * The update page is called Updates and shows what is new in the
    installed version. The storage overview page is gone; its concerns
    are listed above Pools and Disks.
  * A solid colour as background; administrators are asked once for a
    star on GitHub.
  * Firewall changes are confirmed from the browser that made them;
    two-factor attempts are limited per account; certificates are
    replaced in one checked step.
  * Moves between volumes remove only what was copied and written out.
  * Fixes and hardening throughout.

 -- zendiloOS <github.com@stuch.me>  Mon, 09 Nov 2026 14:32:05 +0000

zendiloos (0.2.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * A file explorer replaces the Shares app: folders on a volume with their
    own permissions, shared over SMB and NFS when asked, folder properties,
    previous versions from snapshots, one recycle bin, a deep search.
  * Removable drives (FAT, exFAT, NTFS, ext4) are mounted, browsed and
    ejected by administrators.
  * The App Center installs apps as Docker projects of one's own; the
    Docker app has pages for containers and projects, a live deploy log,
    a terminal into containers and image updates.
  * Plugins: Docker, and an ACME client for Let's Encrypt certificates; the
    web interface's ports, HTTP redirect and certificate, confirmed or
    rolled back.
  * Notifications through one mail server to e-mail and webhook channels,
    chosen per kind.
  * Two-factor sign-in with an authenticator app.
  * A firewall in nftables, Docker's published ports included.
  * The first administrator is created on the first visit, without a code
    on the console.
  * Storage, the resource monitor and the logs move into the control panel.
  * Fixes and hardening from three rounds of audits.

 -- zendiloOS <github.com@stuch.me>  Sun, 08 Nov 2026 11:13:07 +0000

